<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Shun dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1644 CE – 1645 CE · capital Xi'an, Beijing</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The dynasty of the rebel Li Zicheng, whose capture of Beijing in 1644 ended the Ming but opened the way for the Manchu conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>, a dismissed courier from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, became a rebel leader in the 1630s amid famine and military mutiny. Promising to cut taxes and redistribute land, he built a huge army, took Xi'an and early in 1644 proclaimed the dynasty of Great Shun there.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Shun set up ministries and appointed officials across the north, but it governed mainly by military occupation. When Li took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in April 1644 and the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> emperor hanged himself, his troops tortured Ming officials to extract <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, and discipline broke down, alienating the elite.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-sangui">Wu Sangui</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanhai-pass">Shanhai Pass</a> allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>, and their combined forces defeated Li in May 1644. He retreated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> to Xi'an and then into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>, where he was killed in 1645. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> inherited the north.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shun-dynasty.jpg" alt="Shun dynasty" width="320" height="324"><figcaption>Image: DamiKarv · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shun-dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1644 CE – 1645 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xi'an, Beijing</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Li Zicheng</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Mandarin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Shun, Shun (Li Zicheng)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1644&amp;focus=shun-dynasty" aria-label="Shun dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7452 936 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shun dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7772 1192L7643 1242M7772 1192L7567 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7643 1242h0M7567 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7772 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7772 1192h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Xi'an. <a href="/atlas?year=1644&amp;focus=shun-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
